@author "Thomas Luebker"
@node MAIN "amipkg - the AmigaPKG package manager"

@{b}amipkg - the package manager for AmigaOS 3.x@{ub}

Install, update and remove software from a curated, cryptographically
signed catalog of 100+ packages - straight on your Amiga.

  @{" What is amipkg?        " link ABOUT}
  @{" Requirements           " link REQ}
  @{" The command line       " link CLI}
  @{" The GUIs (two!)        " link GUI}
  @{" Adopting existing apps " link ADOPT}
  @{" How the trust works    " link TRUST}
  @{" Troubleshooting        " link TROUBLE}

Catalog + package submissions:
  https://github.com/thomas-luebker/amiga-pkg

(c) 2026 Thomas Luebker. amipkg is part of the Amiga Imager project
(https://amiga-imager.com) but runs on ANY AmigaOS 3.x system.
@endnode

@node ABOUT "What is amipkg?"
@{b}What is amipkg?@{ub}

Think "apt or Homebrew for your A500/A1200/A4000":

 - A curated catalog of 100+ freely distributable classics and tools
   (players, commodities, internet apps, dev tools, games).
 - @{b}Dependency resolution@{ub}: installing an app also installs what it
   needs (MUI, class libraries, ...), picking the right CPU build for
   your machine (68000..68060).
 - @{b}Clean removal@{ub}: a receipt database records every file and every
   User-Startup change; remove deletes only what was installed and
   strips its boot-script blocks.
 - @{b}Self-updating@{ub}: `amipkg upgrade amipkg`.
 - Everything is verified: see @{"How the trust works" link TRUST}.
@endnode

@node REQ "Requirements"
@{b}Two setups@{ub}: plain @{b}Install@{ub} keeps everything in the drawer
(zero system footprint - no assign, no lock; the programs find their
data next to their binaries); @{b}Install-System@{ub} additionally adds an
optional AMIPKG: assign + Shell path to S:User-Startup (one marked
block, pure convenience).

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

 - AmigaOS 3.0 or newer. amipkg itself runs on a stock 68000.
 - C:lha - the LhA unarchiver (standard on almost every system).
 - A TCP/IP stack with bsdsocket.library, ONLY for downloads
   (Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami, or an emulator's built-in bsdsocket).
   Browsing the bundled catalog works offline.
 - Optional: @{b}AmiSSL 5.x@{ub} enables downloads from https-only hosts
   (GitHub releases). Everything on Aminet works without it.
 - Optional: @{b}MUI 3.8+@{ub} for the MUI front-end
   (`amipkg install mui38` bootstraps it).

Some PACKAGES need more (68020+, more RAM, newer Kickstart) - amipkg
checks the requirements and refuses clearly instead of installing
something that cannot run.
@endnode

@node CLI "The command line"
@{b}The command line@{ub}

  amipkg update              fetch the latest signed catalog
  amipkg avail [term]        list / search the catalog
  amipkg info <id>           details for one package
  amipkg install <id>        download + verify + install WITH deps
  amipkg install <id> DRYRUN preview the plan, change nothing
  amipkg adopt <id> <drawer> manage an app you ALREADY have
  amipkg list                what is installed
  amipkg check               which installed packages have updates
  amipkg upgrade [<id>]      update everything (or one package);
                             `amipkg upgrade amipkg` self-updates
  amipkg remove <id>         clean uninstall (receipt-driven)
  amipkg doctor              audit installs against the receipts
  amipkg dir [<path>]        show/set the install drawer
                             (default SYS:Programs)

Commands are case-sensitive: `install`, not `Install`.

Downloads resume after an interruption, and every archive must match
the SHA-256 recorded in the signed catalog or it is refused.
@endnode

@node GUI "The GUIs"
@{b}Two front-ends - pick your flavour@{ub}

@{b}amipkg-mui@{ub} (needs MUI 3.8+)
  The polished one: toolbar, sidebar with View and Categories panes,
  real list columns, description pane and a status bar with catalog
  counters. Everything the CLI can do, point-and-click.

@{b}amipkg-gui@{ub} (GadTools)
  Runs on ANY stock 3.x system with zero dependencies.

Both offer the same features:
 - Installed / Available views, Find box, category filter,
   By Name / Newest sorting.
 - Install shows the resolved plan (dependencies + download sizes)
   BEFORE you confirm.
 - Installs run in the background with live progress - the window
   stays usable.
 - Run button: launch an installed program directly.
 - Update Catalog / Check Updates / Update All, Info (double-click a
   row), Remove, Adopt Existing, Install Drawer - all in the menus.
@endnode

@node ADOPT "Adopting existing apps"
@{b}You already have the app? Adopt it.@{ub}

You installed Visage years ago in Work:Grafik/Visage? Tell amipkg:
(Adopted the wrong drawer? Just adopt again - the old record is
replaced, no files are touched.)

  amipkg adopt visage Work:Grafik/Visage
  amipkg adopt visage Work:Grafik/Visage 39.21   (if you know the version)

amipkg inventories the drawer into its receipt database (with
checksums), links it to the catalog entry, and remembers WHERE it
lives. From then on:

 - `amipkg check` watches it for updates,
 - upgrades land IN PLACE (Work:Grafik/), not in SYS:Programs,
 - `amipkg remove` can cleanly uninstall it.

In the GUIs: select the package, then Package menu ->
"Adopt Existing..." and pick the drawer.
@endnode

@node TRUST "How the trust works"
@{b}How the trust works@{ub}

1. The catalog (packages.json) is signed OFFLINE with the project's
   Ed25519 key. Your Amiga verifies that signature against a built-in
   public key before trusting a single byte - `amipkg update` refuses
   a tampered catalog.

2. Every package archive's SHA-256 is recorded INSIDE that signed
   catalog. Every download must match it or it is deleted.

3. Because of 1+2, the transport does not need to be trusted: plain
   HTTP (which classic Amigas need - no TLS without AmiSSL) is safe,
   and so are mirrors and redirects.

Nothing reaches the catalog unsigned: packages are submitted as pull
requests, reviewed by a maintainer (licensing + any native-code
lines), and only then signed in.
@endnode

@node TROUBLE "Troubleshooting"
@{b}Troubleshooting@{ub}

@{b}"no TCP/IP stack running"@{ub}
  Bring your network up first (GoOnline / your stack), then retry.
  On FS-UAE: enable "UAE bsdsocket.library" in the Launcher.

@{b}"this URL needs https, and AmiSSL is not installed"@{ub}
  That package is hosted on an https-only site (e.g. GitHub).
  Install AmiSSL 5.x (Aminet util/libs/amissl), then retry.

@{b}"not enough space"@{ub}
  amipkg checks free disk space BEFORE downloading. Free some space
  or change the install drawer (`amipkg dir <path>`).

@{b}"needs a 68020 or better" / "needs Kickstart 3.2+"@{ub}
  The package declares hardware/OS floors your machine does not
  meet. That is the check working as intended.

@{b}Moved the amipkg drawer?@{ub}
  Self-contained setup: nothing to do - the data moves with the
  binaries. Integrated setup: delete the old "amipkg home" block
  from S:User-Startup and run Install-System again from there.

@{b}"Cannot delete the amipkg drawer - object in use"@{ub}
  Close any running amipkg program. If you ran Install-System, also:
    Assign AMIPKG: REMOVE
  (Since 0.4.10 amipkg itself creates no assign and holds no lock.)

@{b}A package misbehaves after manual file edits@{ub}
  `amipkg doctor` lists modified/missing files per package;
  `amipkg install <id>` repairs it.

Report bugs: https://github.com/thomas-luebker/amiga-pkg/issues
@endnode
